      "instructions": "11-15: Choose the correct letter for each item.\n16-20: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Mabel Truett: Welcome to the Riverside Community Darkroom. This induction explains where tasks happen and what members do when something goes wrong. It does not qualify anyone to mix concentrated chemicals or repair equipment.\nMabel Truett: The completely light-tight film-loading bench is station A. That is where unexposed film is loaded into a processing tank.\nMabel Truett: The room is organised from dry work to wet processing. Bags and drinks remain outside. Clean hands protect negatives, while dedicated tongs prevent one solution being transferred into another tray.\nMabel Truett: Paper processing starts at the developer station, B. Keep the developer tongs there rather than carrying them to the wash sink.\nMabel Truett: Ventilation operates whenever the wet side is open. A quiet fan sound is normal. If the warning light appears, stop processing and notify staff rather than switching the fan off.\nMabel Truett: Finished prints receive their extended rinse in the wash sink, C, after the fixing stage is complete.\nMabel Truett: Safe lighting reduces exposure for suitable photographic paper, but it is not ordinary room light. Phones, watches and door openings can fog material. Members should know where the covered paper box is before beginning.\nMabel Truett: Labelled negative sleeves are returned through the negative cabinet, D. Loose strips must never be left on a table.\nMabel Truett: Chemical containers stay labelled. Members do not pour used liquid into a sink unless the supervisor directs them to the correct collection point. The club separates working solutions and keeps a log of disposal.\nMabel Truett: Fibre-based prints that are ready for controlled drying go to the print dryer at E after members complete the separate demonstration.\nMabel Truett: Bookings include setup and cleanup. Arriving late does not extend the session because the next member needs the station. Leave trays rinsed, surfaces dry and tools in their marked positions.\nMabel Truett: Book a full session because the stations are shared and overlap creates unsafe crowding. The booking does not guarantee one-to-one teaching.\nMabel Truett: Prints and negatives follow different storage routes. Wet prints remain in the processing area; negatives return in sleeves. A name and date prevents an unclaimed strip being mistaken for waste.\nMabel Truett: If the safe light fails, do not use a phone. Cover light-sensitive materials immediately and call the supervisor from the doorway.\nMabel Truett: Personal chemicals are accepted only in the checked storage locker, with contents and owner clearly labelled.\nMabel Truett: The print dryer becomes available after a dry-mounting demonstration. It does not require another card or a payment each time.\nMabel Truett: Tell the duty supervisor about every spill immediately. Do not hide the report in the calendar or write it only on an envelope.\nMabel Truett: Members should plan the session before the room becomes dark. Set out only the paper and tools needed for the first print, then return unused materials to closed boxes. Searching through a bag under safe light wastes time and risks contamination. The emergency eyewash and spill kit are beside the lit lobby door, accessible without crossing the wet benches. If skin contact occurs, follow the posted first response and call the supervisor; do not wait until cleanup. Test strips and failed paper prints go into the marked solid-waste container, not into chemical-liquid bottles. At the end, note low supplies on the board rather than opening reserve stock. The coordinator checks that request before the next session. These routines support shared use: one person’s hurried cleanup can affect the following member’s negatives and prints.\nMabel Truett: We will now walk through the dry side with the room lights on, then practise the emergency procedure before any materials are opened.",
