      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct letter for each item.\n26-30: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Zachary Lorne: Elspeth, we have an engaging idea, but the tutor said our claim was too confident. Reconstructing a historical smell means combining partial evidence. It does not recover one objective atmosphere unchanged from the past.\nElspeth Faircombe: The trade ledger lists materials the workshop actually purchased, so it can identify substances available even when no mixing instructions survive.\nZachary Lorne: The workshop repaired leather goods, heated wax and stored timber. Each activity could contribute volatile compounds. We need a source table showing what each document supports and where interpretation begins.\nElspeth Faircombe: A recipe inventory gives the language and proportions used to describe a mixture, although we must not assume the workers followed it exactly.\nZachary Lorne: Recipes are tempting because they look precise. Yet an inventory may describe ideal practice rather than daily use, and a ledger proves purchase rather than quantity in the air. Our exhibit text must preserve those distinctions.\nElspeth Faircombe: A material sample taken from a surviving tool can confirm residue, but contamination and later conservation treatment have to be considered.\nZachary Lorne: Material evidence creates another problem. Residue on a tool may be old, recent or introduced by conservation. We can use laboratory advice to narrow possibilities, but not turn an uncertain trace into a complete recipe.\nElspeth Faircombe: An oral history records a remembered sensory impression. It is valuable evidence of experience, not a chemical measurement.\nZachary Lorne: Visitor experience must be optional. Scent can trigger discomfort or strong memory. The unscented route should provide the same historical explanation, so declining the strip does not reduce access to the exhibit.\nElspeth Faircombe: The building survey can locate doors, roof vents and other likely ventilation points, helping us understand how odour may have moved.\nZachary Lorne: We should pilot both the wording and the delivery system. A strong mixture may seem dramatic, but intensity is not evidence of accuracy. Starting low also helps us identify whether visitors can distinguish the target note from the room itself.\nElspeth Faircombe: The label should call the recreated smell an interpretation, not the authentic smell of every workshop day.\nZachary Lorne: For evaluation, we can ask what source visitors think supports the reconstruction. If they assume the scent came directly from a preserved bottle, our interpretation label has failed even if they enjoy the experience.\nElspeth Faircombe: Visitors will not inhale from a bottle. A single-use scent strip gives a controlled optional encounter.\nZachary Lorne: Task allocation should maintain independence. I can build the evidence matrix. Zachary can design the visitor instructions, and our tutor can review the historical-claim language before any public trial.\nElspeth Faircombe: Before participation, one screening question asks about fragrance sensitivity, without demanding private medical details.\nZachary Lorne: The first trial uses a low concentration. We can increase it only if detection is poor and the safety boundary still holds.\nElspeth Faircombe: To separate feedback from names, each response sheet receives an anonymous code that links only to the session condition.\nZachary Lorne: The scent formula itself should be prepared by a qualified partner, not improvised by students from historical ingredients. Our role is to specify the evidence and interpretive goal, while the partner documents modern materials and exposure limits. The exhibition record should preserve both layers: why a note was historically plausible and what safe substitute delivered it today. We also need an unscented control strip during the pilot. Some visitors may report a smell because the label creates an expectation. Comparing coded scented and unscented strips will not tell us whether the reconstruction is historically true, but it will show how instructions shape perception. Questions should avoid asking whether visitors liked the smell before asking what they noticed; preference can dominate later descriptions. A neutral sequence begins with detection, then character, then confidence. Finally, quotes from oral histories require permission and context. We can summarise remembered impressions without presenting one person’s memory as the experience of every worker. That distinction belongs in the evidence matrix and in the public label.\nElspeth Faircombe: With those boundaries, the project becomes a study of evidence and interpretation rather than a theatrical claim of authenticity.",
