      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Snow may look biologically empty, yet seasonal snowfields can support communities of cold-adapted microorganisms. Today we focus on algae that colour the surface and on the cautious way researchers connect biology with energy balance.\nLecturer: Many snow algae persist as dormant cells beneath the winter snowpack, becoming active only when seasonal conditions improve.\nLecturer: Life in snow is constrained by low temperature, intense light, limited nutrients and changing liquid water. The organisms do not grow uniformly through winter. Activity concentrates in periods when microscopic films of water are available.\nLecturer: Growth accelerates when liquid meltwater becomes available around the grains; cold alone does not mean the habitat is completely dry.\nLecturer: Pigments serve biological functions, but they also alter what an observer sees. Colour intensity depends on cell abundance, pigment concentration, grain size, lighting and impurities mixed with the snow.\nLecturer: Some cells produce a protective pigment that screens intense radiation and can make a snow surface appear red or pink.\nLecturer: Albedo links appearance to physics. Fresh bright snow reflects much incoming radiation. A darker surface may absorb more. Measuring that effect requires an instrument, not simply ranking photographs by eye.\nLecturer: Nutrients may arrive with wind-blown dust, but the amount and source differ among sites and storms.\nLecturer: Researchers combine cell counts, pigment measurements and spectral reflectance. Molecular methods help distinguish organisms that have similar shapes, while microscopy still provides information about cell form and condition.\nLecturer: When biological colour darkens the surface, its albedo—the fraction of incoming light reflected—can decrease.\nLecturer: Scale creates a challenge. A handheld instrument measures a small patch accurately. A drone or satellite sees a much larger area but combines biological colour with shadows, dust, rocks and wet snow.\nLecturer: Lower reflection means more energy is absorbed, which can accelerate snowmelt under suitable weather conditions.\nLecturer: Causality also runs in both directions. Algae can darken snow, yet earlier melting can create more liquid water and expose nutrients that support growth. A correlation between colour and melt cannot by itself establish the full sequence.\nLecturer: Researchers sample several colour patches because one red area does not represent the whole community; spatial variation is part of the result.\nLecturer: Climate relevance depends on extent, timing and local conditions. A dark patch may have a strong local effect but cover little area. Conversely, widespread light colouring may have a modest effect at each point.\nLecturer: Satellite colour covers large regions, but mineral dust and other materials can look similar, so colour alone does not uniquely identify algae.\nLecturer: Good field design therefore repeats sampling across elevations and dates and records weather. The goal is not one universal number, but an estimate with clear limits and a mechanism supported by several kinds of evidence.\nLecturer: The feedback is plausible, yet its strength varies among sites. It should not be described as the dominant cause of melting everywhere.\nLecturer: Repeated measurements follow change through the melt season, separating a single snapshot from a developing biological and physical process.\nLecturer: Surface samples must be handled carefully because walking through a coloured patch can mix clean and affected snow. Teams approach from the down-slope side, record the undisturbed appearance and collect replicate cores at defined spacing. Temperature is measured at several depths, since a warm surface can sit above colder layers. Laboratory work estimates cell abundance and pigment composition. Researchers may also measure nutrients and mineral particles to test alternative explanations for colour and growth. A statistical model can then relate albedo to several predictors rather than assigning every darkening effect to algae. Temporal sampling is especially valuable after fresh snowfall. New snow may cover a bloom, raise reflectivity and then reveal colour again as melting proceeds. The observed surface is therefore a moving boundary. Regional estimates add another layer of uncertainty. Cloud shadows and viewing angle influence remote-sensing signals, and validation sites may not represent inaccessible terrain. Reporting an uncertainty range is more informative than a single precise-looking area. Snow algae illustrate a coupled system: organisms respond to melt conditions and may, in turn, modify absorbed energy. The feedback can be studied without claiming that biology controls the entire seasonal snow budget. Physical weather, dust deposition, slope and elevation remain major parts of the explanation. Researchers also archive matched samples so later analytical methods can revisit uncertain identifications. Transparent metadata about collection depth, light conditions and instrument calibration makes that archive scientifically useful rather than merely decorative.\nLecturer: Keep those scale and causality limits in mind when we examine next week’s case study from a maritime glacier.",
