      "passage": "A. Everyday colour names are flexible. One person’s “sage” may be another person’s grey-green, and the same word can migrate between fashion, paint and natural history. This elasticity is useful in conversation but troublesome when a scientist, printer and manufacturer must reproduce a colour rather than evoke it. Standardisation began with a practical recognition: verbal labels need a visible reference. A sample gives observers something to compare, even if it does not eliminate every source of variation.\n\nB. Early systems often arranged painted cards or material chips into an ordered set. Their power came from relation. A sample was not merely “blue”; it occupied a position among lighter, darker, warmer and cooler neighbours. Users could point to an entry and communicate more precisely across distance. Yet handmade samples varied, and pigments changed with age. A reference could stabilise a discussion while slowly becoming less like the colour it was supposed to preserve.\n\nC. Industrial swatches tied colour communication to production. Textile, ceramic and paint makers could compare a new batch with an accepted sample and reject visible departures. This supported contracts and quality control, but it also exposed material differences: a colour on glossy metal may not look identical on woven cloth. Standardisation therefore shifted from naming a hue to defining conditions of comparison. Surface texture, viewing angle and illumination became part of the problem.\n\nD. Two samples can appear to match under one lamp and separate under another because their materials reflect wavelengths differently. Controlled viewing booths address this by providing standard illumination. They do not create a universally “true” appearance; they make the viewing condition reproducible. This distinction is important. An observer may still need to ask how the product will look in daylight, a shop or a home, but comparisons made under the agreed lamp can be repeated by another laboratory.\n\nE. Instruments translate reflected light into numerical readings. Numbers improve record keeping and allow tolerances to be specified, but they do not make physical samples obsolete. Different instruments and settings can produce small differences, and a numerical match may not capture every perceptual effect of texture. Many organisations therefore use instrument readings alongside visual assessment. The two methods answer related but not identical questions: one quantifies a signal; the other judges an appearance in context.\n\nF. A standard needs a history. Samples exposed repeatedly to light, dust and handling may fade or become soiled. A reference archive protects master samples, while working copies absorb daily use. Dates, materials and replacement decisions should be recorded so that a “match” from one decade is not silently compared with a changed reference in the next. Custody sounds administrative, yet without it a carefully designed colour system can drift while retaining the same labels.\n\nG. Colour standards do not abolish perception or context. They create negotiated consistency by specifying a reference, a method and an acceptable range. This is enough to coordinate many scientific and industrial tasks, provided users remember the boundary. A colour sample communicates more than a name, and an instrument reading offers more than an impression, but neither is independent of material, light and purpose. The strongest systems make those conditions visible rather than hiding them behind an apparently exact code.\n\nH. Digital colour files make specifications easy to transmit, but the final product remains physical. A screen emits light, while paint, paper and fabric reflect it. Calibration can improve correspondence between devices, yet it cannot erase this difference. A robust workflow therefore links digital values to controlled proofs and production samples. Each stage narrows uncertainty rather than promising perfect identity. The practical success of a standard is visible when disagreements can be diagnosed: users can ask whether the file, display, illumination, substrate or ageing reference caused the mismatch. Without that chain, numerical precision may only make confusion look authoritative. Communication also needs a tolerance. Demanding an exact numerical identity may create needless waste when a small difference is invisible in normal use, while a generous tolerance can permit an obvious mismatch. The acceptable range should follow the product's purpose, not the convenience of whichever measurement happens to be available.",
