      "passage": "A. After a disaster, needs arrive faster than agencies can inspect them. Roads may be blocked, communications intermittent and records incomplete. Triage is unavoidable: someone must decide which request receives attention first. Algorithms can support prioritisation by combining reports, location and available resources. Their attraction is not that they remove values but that they apply a stated rule quickly across many cases. The danger begins when speed is mistaken for certainty or when the rule’s moral choices are hidden inside technical language.\n\nB. A model can rank only what enters its data. Neighbourhoods with reliable connections may submit more detailed reports than equally affected areas that are offline. If report volume is treated as need, visibility becomes advantage. Missing reports are not evidence of safety. Designers can introduce uncertainty flags or seek alternative signals, but no adjustment fully replaces local knowledge. The output should therefore be read as a provisional map of recorded information, not as a complete picture of suffering.\n\nC. Different objectives produce different queues. Minimising travel time may favour clusters of accessible requests; reducing the greatest immediate risk may send teams farther apart; supporting the largest number of people may disadvantage a small isolated community. None of these calculations is neutral. A priority rule expresses what counts as benefit and whose delay is acceptable. Public agencies should make that rule contestable before a crisis rather than discovering its implications while people are waiting.\n\nD. Allocation decisions alter the data used for later decisions. If a model sends teams to one district, observers there will generate more confirmed reports, which may cause the district to appear even more important. This feedback loop can reinforce an early judgement that was based on incomplete information. Random checks outside the highest-ranked area and deliberate exploration can reveal hidden need. Efficiency measured only where the system already looked may become self-confirming.\n\nE. Frontline coordinators need more than a priority score. A reason code can show which factors drove the result and alert staff to missing or contradictory inputs. Explanation is not merely for public relations; it enables a human reviewer to recognise when a model has interpreted an unusual case badly. However, a long technical account is not automatically useful. Explanations must connect to decisions that a coordinator is authorised and equipped to change.\n\nF. Human oversight becomes ceremonial if staff cannot challenge a ranking. An effective system allows an override, records why it occurred and reviews patterns later. Frequent overrides may reveal flawed inputs, an outdated objective or a local condition that the model does not represent. The log also discourages arbitrary intervention because decisions leave a trace. Accountability therefore runs in both directions: the algorithm should be auditable, and departures from it should be documented.\n\nG. Evaluation should include stress scenarios that reproduce missing data, sudden road closures and competing priorities. Average accuracy on calm historical data says little about performance when the information environment collapses. Teams can examine whether small input changes cause unstable rankings and whether protected groups are systematically overlooked. These tests cannot certify every future event, but they reveal brittle assumptions before those assumptions direct scarce resources.\n\nH. The sensible position is neither automatic trust nor blanket rejection. Algorithmic triage can organise information and expose a consistent priority rule, but responsibility remains institutional. Agencies choose objectives, collect data, set override powers and decide how uncertainty is communicated. A model that accelerates a bad rule is not an improvement. A carefully governed tool, by contrast, can help staff see a crowded situation without pretending to replace judgement. The relevant question is not whether an algorithm decides, but how people design, supervise and answer for its role.\n\nI. Disaster decisions occur under pressure, but affected people still need a way to challenge obvious errors. An appeal channel need not resemble a slow court process. It can allow local teams to flag a closed bridge, duplicated household or medically urgent case that the system missed. The channel should be monitored for unequal access; otherwise well-connected communities may correct their records while isolated ones remain misclassified. Appeals also generate diagnostic evidence. If the same kind of correction appears repeatedly, designers should revise the input process or priority rule instead of treating every case as an exception. Responsiveness, in this sense, improves both fairness and model quality.",
